<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sigismund II Augustus</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/1572">1572</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jagiellonian-dynasty">Jagiellonian dynasty</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sigismund II Augustus was King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> and Grand Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a>, the last of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jagiellonian-dynasty">Jagiellonian dynasty</a>, who died in 1572. He presided over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/union-of-lublin">Union of Lublin</a> of 1569, which formally created the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polish-lithuanian-commonwealth">Polish-Lithuanian Commonwealth</a>. His death without an heir led to the system of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elective-monarchy">elective monarchy</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sigismund-ii-augustus.jpg" alt="Sigismund II Augustus" width="320" height="380" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Lucas Cranach the Younger · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cranach_the_Younger_Sigismund_II_Augustus.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Sigismund Augustus was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krakow">Kraków</a> on 1 August 1520, the elder of two sons of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sigismund-i-the-old">Sigismund I the Old</a> and Bona Sforza of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a>, and the only one to survive infancy. He was educated by the leading scholars in the country. His mother wished to name him Augustus, either for the month of his birth or after the first Roman Emperor.</p><p>Because the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-poland">Polish crown</a> was elective, his parents secured his succession by having him chosen king in his father's lifetime. He was inaugurated as Grand Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a> on 18 October 1529, named King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> alongside his father on 18 December and crowned at the age of nine on 20 February 1530 by Primate Jan Łaski. The nobles criticised this election, which became one of the reasons for the Lwów Rokosz in 1537. He began his reign as active Grand Duke in 1544 and at first opposed the Polish–Lithuanian union, hoping to leave his throne to heirs, since the Lithuanian throne was hereditary and the Polish was not.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>He married Elizabeth of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> on 5 May 1543, when she was 16 and he 22. Queen Bona plotted against her, and the couple settled in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vilnius">Vilnius</a>. Elizabeth died in 1545 at 18. In July or August 1547 he secretly married Barbara Radziwiłł, who was despised by Bona. The deputies of his first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sejm">Sejm</a>, on 31 October 1548, threatened to renounce their allegiance unless he repudiated her, and he even considered abdicating. After his father's death on 1 April 1548 he declared her Queen on 17 April, and she died five months after her coronation. His third wife was Catherine of Austria. Neither marriage produced living children.</p><p>His reign saw the bloodless introduction of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Protestant Reformation</a> into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a>, and the collapse of the Knights of the Sword in the north, which led to the Commonwealth's acquisition of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livonia">Livonia</a> as a Lutheran duchy. He kept a policy of tolerance towards minorities and peaceful relations with neighbours, except for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/northern-seven-years-war">Northern Seven Years' War</a>, fought to secure Baltic trade. He established the first regular Polish navy and the first regular postal service in Poland, and in 1569 oversaw the signing of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/union-of-lublin">Union of Lublin</a>, which formed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polish-lithuanian-commonwealth">Polish–Lithuanian Commonwealth</a>. Culture flourished under his patronage, and he collected tapestries from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/low-countries">Low Countries</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Sigismund Augustus died on 7 July 1572, the last male monarch of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jagiellonian-dynasty">Jagiellonian dynasty</a>. The dynasty ended with the death of his sister Anna in 1596. His rule is widely considered the apex of the Polish Golden Age.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Sigismund II Augustus” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>A privilege from <b>Sigismund II Augustus</b> in 1560 gave it the rights of the Kraków University, making it one of the leading universities of the Polish–Lithuanian Commonwealth.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-prussia">Duchy of Prussia · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1557 the Volok Reform of <b>Sigismund II Augustus</b> reorganised royal estates into standard plots, increasing grain output but tightening serfdom.</blockquote><a href="/empire/grand-duchy-of-lithuania">Grand Duchy of Lithuania · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Sigismund II Augustus</b>, the last male Jagiellonian king, pressed for the union partly to ensure that the joint state would survive the extinction of his line.</blockquote><a href="/empire/polish-lithuanian-commonwealth">Polish-Lithuanian Commonwealth · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In Poland, when the Jagiellonian line ended with the death of <b>Sigismund II Augustus</b> in 1572, the nobles decided that every nobleman who came to the election field near Warsaw should vote.</blockquote><a href="/government/elective-monarchy">Elective monarchy · Through History</a></li><li><blockquote>Hürrem, a captive from Ruthenia, was freed and married by Suleiman around 1534, wrote to King <b>Sigismund II Augustus</b> of Poland and founded a mosque complex in Istanbul with a women’s hospital, opening the age historians call the Sultanate of Women.</blockquote><a href="/societies/ottoman-empire">Social Hierarchy of the Ottoman Empire</a></li><li><blockquote>During the Livonian War the last archbishop, Wilhelm of Brandenburg, placed his lands under Polish-Lithuanian protection, and in 1561-1562 they were secularised and incorporated into the Duchy of Livonia under <b>Sigismund II Augustus</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/archbishopric-of-riga">Archbishopric of Riga</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krakow">Kraków</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/poland">Poland</a>. Died in Knyszyn, in what is now <a href="/countries/poland">Poland</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=50.06,19.94&amp;year=1562" aria-label="Kraków on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Sigismund II Augustus"><image href="/maps/globes/g030n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4450 5804h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4450 5804h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=50.06,19.94&amp;year=1562">What was here in 1562? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
